WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.017
Three helpers can appear in the same chat window and work quite differently.

00:00:05.017 --> 00:00:12.305
A simple question-and-answer chatbot reads your message and explains something it already has in writing.

00:00:12.305 --> 00:00:16.107
Think of asking a receptionist about opening hours.

00:00:16.107 --> 00:00:20.345
A workflow is a recipe someone wrote for a computer.

00:00:20.345 --> 00:00:28.720
It can make choices, like sending an incomplete form back, because a person already specified what to do in each case.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.807
An agent is given a goal and some permitted actions.

00:00:32.807 --> 00:00:37.186
It decides what information to look up next as it learns more.

00:00:37.186 --> 00:00:41.072
Watch what each helper can do for the same shopper.

00:00:41.072 --> 00:00:44.352
Owen bought a jacket and wants a smaller one.

00:00:44.352 --> 00:00:47.153
First he asks about return shipping.

00:00:47.153 --> 00:00:54.630
Orbit’s chatbot reads the shop policy and says shipping is free on orders over seventy-five dollars.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.631
That explains the rule.

00:00:56.631 --> 00:01:04.395
The chatbot in this example has not opened Owen’s order, so it cannot say whether his purchase qualifies.

00:01:04.395 --> 00:01:06.661
So when should you use which?

00:01:06.661 --> 00:01:11.209
If you can write the steps down ahead of time, use a fixed workflow.

00:01:11.209 --> 00:01:17.995
A fixed sequence can be tested with the same starting records and checked against an expected result.

00:01:17.995 --> 00:01:22.071
Reach for an agent when the steps depend on the request.

00:01:22.071 --> 00:01:29.378
Ana wants tracking, Grace wants a return, Owen wants a different size, and you can't predict the mix.

00:01:29.378 --> 00:01:37.265
Letting the model choose a new action can introduce extra calls, expense and opportunities for a mistake.

00:01:37.265 --> 00:01:42.353
So they need limits and testing, and that is a big part of this course.

00:01:42.353 --> 00:01:45.270
Owen submits the shop’s return form.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:49.273
A workflow checks that the required fields are filled in.

00:01:49.273 --> 00:01:52.615
A complete form goes into the returns queue.

00:01:52.615 --> 00:01:56.363
A missing order number sends the form back to Owen.

00:01:56.363 --> 00:02:02.380
This is still a workflow: both routes were written down before Owen bought his jacket.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.523
Follow a parcel through a different fixed workflow at Orbit.

00:02:06.523 --> 00:02:11.923
When Sam's warehouse marks an order as shipped, the orders system announces it.

00:02:11.923 --> 00:02:16.972
A template fills in the customer's name, the item and the tracking number.

00:02:16.972 --> 00:02:19.720
And the email goes out to the customer.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:27.403
Same three steps, every time, in the same order, with nothing to decide, so no language model is needed.

00:02:27.403 --> 00:02:36.692
Once those three steps are connected, a warehouse event can send a useful tracking email without someone deciding the route again.

00:02:36.692 --> 00:02:41.038
Now Owen asks whether he can actually get the smaller jacket.

00:02:41.038 --> 00:02:46.276
Scout must find which jacket he bought before it knows which size to check.

00:02:46.276 --> 00:02:50.748
If there is stock, it can offer the exchange under the shop’s rules.

00:02:50.748 --> 00:02:55.082
If the size is sold out, it can explain the other choices.

00:02:55.082 --> 00:03:02.806
One customer needed a policy answer, a fixed process, and a decision based on fresh information.

00:03:02.806 --> 00:03:07.896
Cover the names of the three helpers and listen to Owen's questions again.

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:10.826
Could a printed sign answer the first one?

00:03:10.826 --> 00:03:12.086
Yes.

00:03:12.086 --> 00:03:17.431
The shipping rule can be written once and explained to the next customer who asks.

00:03:17.431 --> 00:03:19.932
Now cover the name of the jacket.

00:03:19.932 --> 00:03:26.462
The same validation rule still works because the missing field determines the next action.

00:03:26.462 --> 00:03:32.232
Changing the customer's wording does not change where an incomplete form goes.

00:03:32.232 --> 00:03:34.946
Finally, cover the stock result.

00:03:34.946 --> 00:03:37.908
You cannot promise Owen an exchange yet.

00:03:37.908 --> 00:03:44.159
The next useful action depends on which jacket he owns and what the warehouse finds.

00:03:44.159 --> 00:03:48.853
That missing fact is the clue to who should handle the request.
